An AUDIT, not code, answering the three things the owner asked for.

METHOD. 475 corpus repos classified by top-level manifest; a language-
stratified sample of 55 run through the shipped verify-claims on merged dev;
each repo's uncatalogued list recomputed from its own survey using the gate's
OWN functions rather than a reimplementation. 42 of 55 produced a survey --
the 13 excluded are the LARGEST repos, which reach MORE modules, so every
count is a LOWER BOUND and says so.

(1) DENOMINATOR: tens of modules per language, not thousands. Rust -- the
language this item was filed about -- reaches 33.

(2) EVIDENCE BAR: five rules from python.yaml's 76 entries. THE BAR IS NOT MET
BY READING: the python audit's first draft got 5 of 32 verdicts wrong, every
error on the false-all-clear side, caught only by a mechanical probe that is
python-specific and does not port. That turns "which language first" into a
question about locally available toolchains.

(3) THE FINDING THAT REFRAMES THE ITEM. qualifying_only is `not unknown`
(verified in code at verify_claims.py:2960, not from a docstring), so a repo's
list must reach EXACTLY zero; and io_primitives stays stdlib-scoped by standing
ruling, so any third-party entry needs a per-project overlay too. Of 42 repos,
2 are flippable by this work alone and 28 need an overlay as well. Python is
the control that proves it: the one language whose audit is DONE still
contributes 243 uncatalogued entries across 16 repos -- 75 stdlib, 168
third-party.

Files two things it found and dedupes against what was already filed:
INV-zimud (new, P1) is the COVERAGE-GATE residual of INV-funuf, which is
correctly satisfied for its own CLASSIFICATION statement; swift's
variable-name-in-module-slot half is already INV-kotob and is cited, not
refiled.

Corrects two of its own numbers before publishing: a Go classifier that split
on "." before "/" reported every third-party Go module as stdlib (151/0 ->
53/97), and a module reached by two languages was credited to whichever wrote
last, putting python's already-enumerated `math` on python's list.

